  "prediction": "The President's budget for FY2020, released in March 2019, consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The President's submission is required by law to be submitted to Congress by the first Monday in February, but incoming administrations typically do not release the multivolume budget sets until later. Other related budget documents include the annual Economic Report of the President and the CBO's Budget and Economic Outlook.\n\nThe main budget document, the annual budget volume, contains information on the President's new budget proposals and summaries of government activities. Summary tables provide projections of budget baselines, receipts, and outlays for the next ten years. The Major Savings and Reforms document outlines the Administration's proposed cost savings measures.\n\nThe budget documents are available online from both OMB and the Government Publishing Office (GPO). The budget documents can also be purchased in print. The budget documents cover a wide range of topics, including economic analysis, aid to state and local governments, and historical budget data.\n\nAfter the President's budget submission, Congress begins its appropriations process, which includes hearings, markups, and floor action on the 12 regular appropriations bills. The Congressional Research Service provides web resources for Members of Congress and the public on the budget and appropriations process.\n\nIn summary, the President's budget for FY2020 consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget. The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The budget documents are available online and in print, and the appropriations process begins after the President's submission.",
  "scored_prediction": "The President's budget for FY2020, released in March 2019, consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB). The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The President's submission is required by law to be submitted to Congress by the first Monday in February, but incoming administrations typically do not release the multivolume budget sets until later. Other related budget documents include the annual Economic Report of the President and the CBO's Budget and Economic Outlook.\n\nThe main budget document, the annual budget volume, contains information on the President's new budget proposals and summaries of government activities. Summary tables provide projections of budget baselines, receipts, and outlays for the next ten years. The Major Savings and Reforms document outlines the Administration's proposed cost savings measures.\n\nThe budget documents are available online from both OMB and the Government Publishing Office (GPO). The budget documents can also be purchased in print. The budget documents cover a wide range of topics, including economic analysis, aid to state and local governments, and historical budget data.\n\nAfter the President's budget submission, Congress begins its appropriations process, which includes hearings, markups, and floor action on the 12 regular appropriations bills. The Congressional Research Service provides web resources for Members of Congress and the public on the budget and appropriations process.\n\nIn summary, the President's budget for FY2020 consists of a multi-volume set of materials issued by the Office of Management and Budget. The budget documents include information on new proposals, summary tables, economic analysis, and supporting documents. The budget documents are available online and in print, and the appropriations process begins after the President's submission.",
